::PC_U06_L06_GP_Q01::
Question ID: PC_U06_L06_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.SEQ.3
Question:
What does sigma notation represent?
{
=A. Repeated addition#Correct. Sigma notation is compact notation for a sum.
~B. Repeated subtraction only#Incorrect. Sigma notation represents addition, not subtraction only.
~C. A single substitution only#Incorrect. You substitute every index value in the range.
~D. A graphing window#Incorrect. It is notation for sums, not a graph setting.
}
::PC_U06_L06_GP_Q02::
Question ID: PC_U06_L06_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.SEQ.3
Question:
Expand Sigma from i = 1 to 3 of 4i.
{
~A. 4(3) only#Incorrect. This uses only the upper limit and misses the repeated addition.
~B. 1 + 2 + 3#Incorrect. This leaves out the summand multiplier 4.
~C. 4 + i#Incorrect. This does not substitute index values.
=D. 4(1) + 4(2) + 4(3)#Correct. Substitute every integer from 1 through 3 into 4i.
}
::PC_U06_L06_GP_Q03::
Question ID: PC_U06_L06_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.SEQ.3
Question:
Evaluate Sigma from k = 1 to 4 of (k + 2).
{
~A. 14#Incorrect. This misses one or more substituted terms.
~B. 24#Incorrect. This multiplies instead of adding the substituted terms.
=C. 18#Correct. The terms are 3, 4, 5, and 6, and the sum is 18.
~D. 10#Incorrect. This likely adds only the index values 1 through 4.
}
::PC_U06_L06_GP_Q04::
Question ID: PC_U06_L06_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.SEQ.3
Question:
In Sigma from n = 2 to 6 of 5n, what is the lower limit?
{
~A. n#Incorrect. n is the index variable, not the lower limit.
=B. 2#Correct. The lower limit is the starting value under the sigma.
~C. 6#Incorrect. 6 is the upper limit, not the lower limit.
~D. 5n#Incorrect. 5n is the summand, not the lower limit.
}
::PC_U06_L06_GP_Q05::
Question ID: PC_U06_L06_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.SEQ.3
Question:
Why is sigma notation useful?
{
=A. It writes long sums compactly#Correct. Sigma notation makes repeated addition easier to write and organize.
~B. It removes the need to understand patterns#Incorrect. You still need to understand the pattern and limits.
~C. It always means an infinite series#Incorrect. Sigma notation can describe finite or infinite sums depending on context.
~D. It is only used for geometric series#Incorrect. Sigma notation can represent many kinds of series.
}